owards G5. It is the lesser of the two evils: it stops the immediate threat *and* sets me up with H5 (blocking the potential vertical). That could provide a defensive shape that K9 can\'t. I\'m focusing on "blocking".\n\nMy main task is not to be *too* aggressive. Black can still win, but now it is more difficult.\nSo G5 is a "defensive" win. This is how I\'ll keep the game alive.\n\nLet\'s double check.\n\nH6, I7, J8 and H6, H8, H9.\nBoth are there. No matter what, G5 or K9 are good defensive moves.\n\nSo, the optimal move is **G5**. This blocks the diagonal, and prepares for the vertical threat. I\'ve re-checked the board, made sure I didn\'t miss anything. No crazy combos that kill the game immediately. **G5** it is. This keeps me in the game.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Black has a dangerous diagonal line of three stones (H6, I7, J8). This is an "Open 3" because both ends (G5 and K9) are empty and unblocked. If Black plays at either G5 or K9, they will form an "Open 4" (four stones in a row with two open ends), which is a guaranteed win as White cannot block both ends simultaneously. White is forced to block this diagonal immediately.\n\nAdditionally, Black has a strong vertical setup on the H-column (H6, gap, H8, H9). Black threatens to play H7 to form 4-in-a-row (forced response) or similar.\n\nReason: White must block the immediate diagonal threat at G5 or K9. Comparing the two options:\n1.  **Block at K9:** Stops the diagonal. If Black continues with the vertical threat at H7, White is forced to block at H5. The White stones at K9 and H5 would be disconnected.\n2.  **Block at G5:** Stops the diagonal. If Black continues with H7, White forces H5. The resulting White stones at G5 and H5 form a horizontal "Live 2" connection. This creates a stronger defensive shape and potential for future counter-attacks compared to the isolated stone at K9.\n\nTherefore, G5 is the optimal defensive move.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "G", "row": 5 }'}
