eates some possibilities.\n\nLet me think… I can\'t let Black take a "Live 4". Playing F7 would just delay, but not block the threat. Playing J7 prevents the Black\'s vertical combination. It’s a slightly active defense, which will be the better overall move.\n\nThere are no double threats for me, and no forced win yet. This is about preventing a loss and gaining a slight advantage for the future. So I must block the direct threat. J7 is the better spot to do this.\n\nI\'m solid on this now. I will block at J7. This isolates the Black\'s row and puts me in a better position strategically. Final answer, J7. I am ready to input the move: {"col": "J", "row": 7}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current board state presents a critical threat from Black. Black has formed a horizontal "Live 3" (three stones in a row with open ends) at coordinates G7, H7, and I7. If White does not block this line immediately, Black can play at either F7 or J7 on the next turn to create a "Live 4" (four in a row with open ends), which is an indefensible winning position. White does not have any immediate 4-in-a-row threats or VCF (Victory by Continuous Fours) sequences to ignore this threat. Therefore, White is forced to block on Row 7.\n\nReason:\nWhite must block at either F7 or J7.\n- **Option F7:** Blocks the row but is relatively isolated from other White stones and leaves the right side open for Black to connect with their stone at J8.\n- **Option J7:** Blocks the row and is strategically better. J7 is adjacent to Black\'s stone at J8; occupying it prevents Black from creating vertical synergy or a "cluster" of stones on the right side. Additionally, playing J7 creates a diagonal connection with White\'s existing stone at I8, improving White\'s shape for potential counter-play.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "J", "row": 7 }'}
