ential threat on Row 6 and extend White\'s diagonal I8-J7. However, it is a defensive/prophylactic move and yields the initiative to Black.\n\nPlaying J5 is the most aggressive and sound strategy. It forces Black to play K5, restricting Black\'s options and keeping the initiative with White. After the exchange (White J5, Black K5), White can continue to build attacks (e.g., at E7 or K6) while Black is forced to defend.\n\nReason: White has a line of 3 stones on Row 5 (G5, H5, I5). Playing at J5 creates a 4-in-a-row threat. This is a "Sente" move, meaning it forces the opponent to defend immediately (at K5) or lose the game. Maintaining the initiative is crucial in Gomoku, and this move allows White to dictate the flow of the game.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 5}'}
